Step-by-Step: Enable PackageHub + Add PostgreSQL 18 Repository
1. Check current registration status
Bashsudo transactional-update register --status
sudo SUSEConnect --status-text
2. List available extensions/modules
Bashsudo transactional-update register --list-extensions
# or
sudo SUSEConnect --list-extensions
3. Enable PackageHub (requires valid SCC registration code)
Run this inside a transactional-update session:
Bashsudo transactional-update register -p PackageHub/15.7/x86_64
(Replace 15.7 with your actual base version if different — SLE Micro 6.2 is based on SLE 15 SPx technology.)
If you get a version mismatch, first register the base product if needed:
Bashsudo transactional-update register -r YOUR_SCC_REGISTRATION_CODE
4. Add the official PostgreSQL PGDG Repository
Bashsudo rpm --import https://zypp.postgresql.org/keys/PGDG-RPM-GPG-KEY-SLES15

sudo zypper install https://download.postgresql.org/pub/repos/zypp/reporpms/SLES-15.7-x86_64/pgdg-suse-repo-latest.noarch.rpm
5. Refresh repositories and install PostgreSQL 18
Bashsudo transactional-update pkg install postgresql18-server postgresql18-contrib
Then reboot the system for the transaction to take effect:
Bashsudo reboot
6. After reboot – Initialize and start PostgreSQL
Bashsudo postgresql-setup --initdb
sudo systemctl enable --now postgresql

Alternative One-Liner Approach (if PackageHub is already enabled)
If you already enabled PackageHub successfully, you can try directly:
Bashsudo transactional-update pkg install postgresql18-server postgresql18-contrib

After installation, verify with:
Bashpsql --version
systemctl status postgresql
